# Izhikevich neuron model
# #######################
#
# Description
# +++++++++++
#
# Implementation of the simple spiking neuron model introduced by Izhikevich [1]_. The dynamics are given by:
#
# .. math::
#
#    \frac{dV_\mathrm{m}}{dt} &= 0.04 V_\mathrm{m}^2 + 5 V_\mathrm{m} + 140 - U_\mathrm{m} + I + s\\
#    \frac{dU_\mathrm{m}}{dt} &= a (b V_\mathrm{m} - U_\mathrm{m})
#
# where
#
# .. math::
#
#    s(t) = \sum_i w_i \delta(t - t_i)
#
# is the weighted input spike train, here modelled as a series of Dirac delta pulses, and :math:`I` is an external input (stimulus) current. Incoming spikes thus cause an instantaneous jump in the membrane potential proportional to the synaptic weight.
#
# The output spikes of the neuron are described by the condition:
#
# .. math::
#
#    &\text{if}\;\; V_\mathrm{m} \geq V_\mathrm{th}:\\
#    &\;\;\;\; V_\mathrm{m} \text{ is set to } c\\
#    &\;\;\;\; U_\mathrm{m} \text{ is incremented by } d\\
#
# As published in [1]_, the numerics differs from the standard forward Euler technique in two ways:
#
# 1. the new value of :math:`U_\mathrm{m}` is calculated based on the new value of :math:`V_\mathrm{m}`, rather than the previous value
# 2. the variable :math:`V_\mathrm{m}` is updated using a time step half the size of that used to update variable :math:`U_\mathrm{m}`.
#
# This model will instead be simulated using the numerical solver that is recommended by ODE-toolbox during code generation.
#
# References
# ++++++++++
#
# .. [1] Izhikevich, Simple Model of Spiking Neurons, IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks (2003) 14:1569-1572
#
